WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
Cold-related illnesses can be just as dangerous as heat-related ones, and they typically fall

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.640
into two categories, frostbite and hypothermia.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.720
Imagine you find a child at a playground who is soaked and shivering after falling into

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.840
a creek.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.520
Knowing exactly how to react is vital.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.240
First, you'll want to check for frostbite, which is the actual freezing of the skin and

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.840
the tissue underneath.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.340
This most often happens to fingers, toes, the nose, and ears.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.740
Look for skin that appears waxy or discolored, or if the area feels hard to move.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.779
Your immediate care involves moving the person to a warm, dry place and replacing any wet

00:00:40.779 --> 00:00:43.599
clothing with dry clothes and blankets.

00:00:43.599 --> 00:00:48.400
If you use water to warm the affected part, make sure it is warm, not hot, and never rub

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.759
the area, as this can cause further tissue damage.

00:00:51.759 --> 00:00:56.259
Keep the person lying flat, limit their movement, and always handle them gently.

00:00:56.259 --> 00:01:00.919
The situation becomes even more critical if the person develops hypothermia, which is

00:01:00.919 --> 00:01:05.360
when the body's core temperature drops because it's losing heat faster than it can produce

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.360
it.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.279
You should be very concerned if the person's shivering stops, if their breathing becomes

00:01:10.279 --> 00:01:15.279
slow or irregular, or if they seem confused and slow to respond.

00:01:15.279 --> 00:01:20.680
If you see these signs, follow the same warming steps we just discussed, but call 911 right

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.680
away.

00:01:21.699 --> 00:01:26.559
Treat the person for shock and stay prepared to start CPR immediately if they become unresponsive

00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:27.400
and stop breathing.

